<h2 style="color: #1a5276; text-align: center; background-color: #e6f2f8; padding: 10px; border-radius: 8px;">Lesson Planner – Theme # I Can Count! – Lesson # 5</h2>
<p style="text-align: center; margin-top: 5px; margin-bottom: 20px;"><em>Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</em></p>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
  <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
    <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 5</td>
    <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Mediation</td>
  </tr>
  <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
    <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> Kinder (CEFR: A1.2)</td>
    <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> Hold Up Ten Fingers!</td>
  </tr>
  <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
    <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> I Can Count!</td>
    <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
  </tr>
  <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
    <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
  </tr>
  <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
    <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2">
      <b>Specific Objective:</b> <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Mediation: concept: Use gestures and drawings to link daily routines to classroom activities that are performed.</p>
      <b>Learning Outcome:</b> 
      <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Can use gestures or drawings to represent numbers (e.g., showing five objects in a drawing).</li>
        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Can use fingers to show numbers up to ten (e.g., holding up three fingers).</li>
        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Can clarify numbers when counting by asking a yes/no question supported with gestures (e.g., asking for repetition by using their fingers: 'Three or four?').</li>
      </ul>
    </td>
  </tr>
</table>

<h3 style="color: #1a5276; text-align: center; margin-top: 20px; margin-bottom: 10px;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h3>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
  <thead>
    <tr style="background-color: #f2f2f2;">
      <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 30%; color: #1a5276;">Stage & Description</th>
      <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%; color: #1a5276;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
      <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 20%; color: #1a5276;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
    </tr>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <h4 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</h4>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification</p>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher leads a "Ten Little Fingers" song with Total Physical Response (TPR). Students sing along and hold up the correct number of fingers as they count from one to ten. This activates prior knowledge of numbers and finger gestures. The teacher emphasizes the initial /f/ sound in 'fingers'.</p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher shows a set of objects (e.g., three red blocks, five yellow pencils) and slowly counts them aloud, holding up the corresponding number of fingers for each count. "One block, two blocks, three blocks!" (showing three fingers). "What number is this?" (showing five fingers). The teacher models how to show numbers with fingers and how to count objects. The initial /k/ sound in 'count' is reinforced.</p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b> The teacher uses simple questions with gestures to check understanding.
          <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">(Teacher shows two fingers) "Is this number three?" (Students respond: "No!")</li>
            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">(Teacher holds up four fingers) "How many fingers?" (Students respond: "Four!")</li>
          </ul>
        </p>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: center; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">___ / ___</p>
        <p style="text-align: center; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(5-7 min)</p>
      </td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <h4 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 2 — Presentation</h4>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "Hold Up Ten Fingers!"</p>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> Large flashcards/posters showing numbers 1-10 with corresponding illustrations of hands holding up fingers and groups of classroom objects (e.g., '1' with one finger and one red block, '5' with five fingers and five yellow pencils). Real objects (blocks, pencils, crayons, markers, books).</p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Students will use gestures and visual aids (drawings) to link numerical concepts to physical representation. This helps mediate the understanding of quantity through non-verbal and visual means.</p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b></p>
        <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Point and Show:</b> The teacher calls out a number (e.g., "Show me number seven!"), and students point to the corresponding flashcard and hold up seven fingers.</li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Match the Quantity:</b> The teacher holds up a flashcard with a number (e.g., '3'). Students must select three real objects (e.g., three blue crayons) and place them next to the flashcard. "Look! Three crayons!"</li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>"How Many?" Guess:</b> The teacher places a small group of objects (e.g., 4 green markers) under a cloth. The teacher lifts the cloth for a quick peek and asks, "How many markers?" Students guess the number and show fingers.</li>
        </ul>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: center; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">___ / ___</p>
        <p style="text-align: center; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(10-12 min)</p>
      </td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <h4 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</h4>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Mediation.</p>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Students practice using gestures (fingers) and drawing to represent numbers and clarify quantities.
          <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Grammar:</b> Quantity + nouns ('three blocks'), Wh-questions ('How many fingers?'), Yes/No questions ('Is this number five?').</li>
            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Vocabulary:</b> Numbers (1-10), hand, finger, block, pencil, book, crayon, marker, glue, count, show, point, hold, match, red, yellow, blue, green, orange, purple.</li>
            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Pronunciation:</b> Initial /k/ in 'count', /f/ in 'fingers'; recognizing number sounds.</li>
          </ul>
        </p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
        <ul style="list-style-type: decimal; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Controlled Practice - "Count and Show":</b> The teacher calls out a number (1-10) or holds up a number card. Students respond by holding up the correct number of fingers and saying the number aloud. The teacher also shows a quantity of objects (e.g., seven books) and asks, "How many books?" Students count and say the number, showing fingers.</li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Semi-controlled - "Object Match and Describe":</b> Students receive individual number cards (1-10) and a tray of mixed classroom objects (blocks, pencils, crayons). They match their number card to the correct quantity of objects. Then, they show their match to the teacher and describe it, e.g., "This is number four, and these are four orange pencils" (while holding up four fingers).</li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Pair Interaction - "Clarify My Count":</b> Students work in pairs. Student A holds up a certain number of fingers (e.g., three). Student B identifies the number, "Three fingers!" Then, Student A might deliberately show a different number or pretend confusion, "Is it three or four?" (showing three fingers then quickly four). Student B must clarify using gestures and a Yes/No question, "No, it's three!" or "Is this three?" (showing three fingers). This encourages clarifying numbers with gestures.</li>
        </ul>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: center; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">___ / ___</p>
        <p style="text-align: center; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(12-15 min)</p>
      </td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <h4 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</h4>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "Hold Up Ten Fingers!"</p>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> Collaborative "Counting Book" Creation and Peer Presentation.</p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to Theme and Scenario:</b> Students will create a page for their class "Counting Book" by drawing objects and matching them to a number. They will then mediate their work by explaining and demonstrating their page using gestures (fingers) to a classmate.</p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
        <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Each student receives a blank page for the "Counting Book" with a pre-assigned number (1-10) written at the top.</li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students are instructed to draw the correct number of objects on their page, using various colored crayons or markers (e.g., 'Page 6': draw six purple flowers; 'Page 2': draw two tall blocks). They can also use glue to attach small cut-out pictures if available.</li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Once drawings are complete, students pair up. Each student takes turns presenting their "Counting Book" page to their partner. They must hold up their page, show the number of fingers corresponding to their number, and say, "Look! I have [number] [objects]!" (e.g., "Look! I have five pencils!" while showing five fingers).</li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The partner is encouraged to ask a clarifying question, "Is that number five?" or "How many pencils?" and the presenter uses gestures to respond. Teachers circulate, prompting with "What color is your block?" or "How many short pencils?". This stage emphasizes turn-taking and active listening.</li>
        </ul>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: center; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">___ / ___</p>
        <p style="text-align: center; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(10-12 min)</p>
      </td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <h4 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</h4>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment strategy.</p>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Students will individually present their "Counting Book" page to the teacher. They must show their drawing, use their fingers to represent the number, and make a simple statement about the quantity. The teacher will ask a clarifying question, prompting the student to use gestures for confirmation or correction.</p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria (Observation Checklist):</b></p>
        <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Can use gestures (fingers) to show numbers 1-10.</li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Can link drawings of objects to the correct number.</li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Responds to 'How many?' questions with a simple number (e.g., 'Three pencils').</li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Attempts to clarify numbers using gestures when prompted (e.g., "Three or four?" with fingers).</li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Demonstrates basic turn-taking and active listening during peer interactions.</li>
        </ul>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students will complete a quick self-reflection.
          <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">(Teacher shows a picture of fingers counting) "Can you show numbers with your fingers? (Draw a happy face / sad face)"</li>
            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">(Teacher shows a picture of a child drawing) "Can you draw numbers in your book? (Draw a happy face / sad face)"</li>
          </ul>
        </p>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: center; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">___ / ___</p>
        <p style="text-align: center; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(5-7 min)</p>
      </td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <h4 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 6 — Reflection</h4>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection:</b></p>
        <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was easy today when we counted and showed numbers?"</li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was tricky about showing numbers to your friend?"</li>
        </ul>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b></p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">The "Counting Book" project successfully engaged students in mediating numerical concepts through drawing and gestures. Most students were able to correctly draw quantities and show corresponding fingers. The clarification activity with 'Three or four?' was challenging for some but provided good practice for the Mediation skill. I need to continue reinforcing the 'How many?' and 'Is this number X?' questions with strong TPR for younger learners. Students enjoyed using colorful crayons (red, yellow, blue, green, orange, purple) for their drawings, which kept them motivated. The initial /k/ and /f/ sounds were well-integrated through the 'count' and 'fingers' activities. Turn-taking was generally observed during partner work. For future lessons, I will provide more guided practice on asking yes/no questions for clarification.</p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson in the theme sequence:</b> This lesson concludes the "I Can Count!" theme. The next thematic unit will build on number recognition and counting, potentially integrating new vocabulary and more complex scenarios for object identification and comparison, further developing their understanding of quantity and descriptive adjectives (tall, small, short, big).</p>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: center; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">___ / ___</p>
        <p style="text-align: center; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(3-5 min)</p>
      </td>
    </tr>
  </tbody>
</table>